# Method 3 — the Ensembl gene set of every approved WikiPathways pathway.
#
# Endpoint : https://sparql.wikipathways.org/sparql
# Release  : WikiPathways RDF 20260810 (pav:createdOn 2026-08-10T00:49:27Z) — verified by
#            analysis/method3/m3_13_genesets.py before any row is used.
# Used by  : analysis/method3/m3_13_genesets.py -> data/method3/derived/method3_pathway_genes.tsv
#
# Method 3 assigns pathways to Key Events, not genes. The gene set of a KE is therefore the
# union of the gene sets of its approved pathways, and this query supplies that membership.
#
# Why not take the genes from the builder's own GMT export, which already carries them?
#
#   1. The GMT carries HGNC *symbols*. Symbols are ambiguous and drift between releases, so
#      harmonising them to Ensembl would introduce a mapping loss that belongs to the symbol
#      round-trip rather than to Method 3. WikiPathways stores the Ensembl cross-reference
#      directly (wp:bdbEnsembl), so asking the source removes a whole error term.
#   2. The builder's corpus is an unpinned snapshot (upstream molAOP-builder #263, open). Going
#      to WikiPathways directly lets the release be pinned and the drift between the two be
#      measured rather than absorbed silently.
#
# The GMT is still fetched and compared — see the drift block in stage 13. The two disagreeing
# is a reportable fact about Method 3's reproducibility, not an error to paper over.
#
# ?wpid is bound by VALUES from the approved set, in batches, rather than by scanning every
# pathway in the endpoint.
#
# Three traps in this query, all of which silently change the numbers rather than erroring:
#
#   * The node class must be wp:DataNode, NOT wp:GeneProduct. WikiPathways types a node by how
#     the author drew it, so the same gene is a GeneProduct on one map and a Protein on another,
#     and the two classes do not nest predictably. WP4324 (Mitochondrial complex I assembly)
#     carries 56 Ensembl-bearing nodes, every one of them wp:Protein and NONE wp:GeneProduct —
#     restricted to GeneProduct it returns an empty gene set and the pathway looks like a
#     metabolite map. WP3959 splits 9 GeneProduct against 53 Protein. wp:DataNode is the
#     superclass of both, and wp:bdbEnsembl does the real filtering: a metabolite has no
#     Ensembl cross-reference, so nothing non-genic enters through the wider class.
#   * wp:organismName must be constrained to "Homo sapiens". WikiPathways holds orthologous
#     pathways for other species under their own identifiers, and a pathway can appear more
#     than once across the loaded graphs; without the constraint a human KE picks up
#     non-human gene products.
#   * rdfs:label is NOT unique per Ensembl identifier — ENSG00000129965 carries both "INS" and
#     "Insulin", ENSG00000113302 both "IL12B" and "IL23B". Counting rows counts labels, not
#     genes. Always aggregate on ?ensembl; the label is carried only to compare against the
#     builder's symbol-based GMT.

PREFIX wp:      <http://vocabularies.wikipathways.org/wp#>
PREFIX dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/>
PREFIX dc:      <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/>
PREFIX rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>

SELECT DISTINCT ?wpid ?title ?ensembl ?symbol
WHERE {
  VALUES ?wpid { __WPIDS__ }

  ?pw a wp:Pathway ;
      dcterms:identifier ?wpid ;
      dc:title ?title ;
      wp:organismName "Homo sapiens" .

  ?gp a wp:DataNode ;
      dcterms:isPartOf ?pw ;
      wp:bdbEnsembl ?ensembl .

  OPTIONAL { ?gp rdfs:label ?symbol }
}
